 Lemma, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines; translating these lines back gives \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k'\\).\n\n   Thus we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   Since a configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Hence the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\), all of which have been shown to be attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad m\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_m\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\) points; it is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(m,1),(1,m)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny covering of a triangle)\n\n*For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe argue by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n\\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). Any line through \\((1,1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) is sunny; one such line suffices.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge 2\\)).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_m:\\; y=(m-1)x+1-(m-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(m-1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_m\\) is sunny.  \n\\(L_m\\) passes through the point \\((1,m)\\in T_m\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=m\\).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) lies on \\(L_m\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb=(m-1)a+1-(m-1)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b-a=m-2a+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\), the only points of \\(T_m\\) that satisfy this equation are those with \\(a=1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nT_m\\cap L_m=\\{(1,m)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other points of \\(T_m\\) have first coordinate at least \\(2\\).  \nIf we delete the first column (\\(a=1\\)) from the picture of \\(T_m\\) and shift the remaining points one unit to the left, we obtain exactly the set \\(T_{m-1}\\). Formally,\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid a\\ge 2\\}= \\{(a-1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{m-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(T_{m-1}\\) can be covered by \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines; translating those lines one unit to the right yields \\(m-1\\) sunny lines covering the points of \\(T_m\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\).\n\nAdding the line \\(L_m\\) gives a family of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains every point of \\(T_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Reduction to a smaller triangle)\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nThe set of points that remain uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}\\{x=i\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate this set by the vector \\((-(n-k),0)\\) we obtain exactly \\(T_k\\). Hence, after the translation, the uncovered part is a triangle of size \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (4). They are distinct and none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   By Lemma&nbsp;1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   Translate each of these \\(k\\) lines by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the translated lines are still sunny (parallelism is preserved) and they cover exactly the set of points left uncovered after step 1.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines. This furnishes a configuration for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nA configuration consists of precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot be larger than \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Consequently the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\). The construction above shows that each of these values actually occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
