ers all of \\(T_m\\), and the count is exactly \\(m\\).\n\n*Overall Lemma 1:* Proven (modulo the minor gap noted).\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Lemma 2 – Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\n> “Consider the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\). These lines are distinct and each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.”\n\n*Correct.* Vertical lines are indeed non‑sunny.\n\n> “Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (3).”\n\n*Correct.* Direct consequence of the definition of the vertical lines.\n\n> “The points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by (3) are precisely  \n> \\(U_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).”\n\n*Correct.* Complement of the covered set within \\(T_n\\).\n\n> “Define the translation \\(\\tau:(a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\).  \n> For \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) we have \\(1\\le a-(n-k)\\le k\\) and  \n> \\((a-(n-k))+b = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1\\).  \n> Thus \\(\\tau(U_{n,k})=T_k\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \nThe equality \\(\\tau(U_{n,k})=T_k\\) is asserted without explicitly showing both inclusions.  \n*Resolution (not required for grading):*  \n- *Subset*: For any \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\), the inequalities above give \\((a-(n-k),b)\\in T_k\\).  \n- *Superset*: Conversely, given \\((a',b)\\in T_k\\), set \\(a=a'+(n-k)\\); then \\(a\\) satisfies \\(n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\). Hence the mapping is bijective.\n\nThus Lemma 2 is valid.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (0 ≤ k ≤ n)\n\n*Step 1 – Non‑sunny lines*  \n\n> “Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3).”\n\n*Correct.* Provides the required number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Step 2 – Sunny lines*  \n\n> “By Lemma 1, the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  \n> Translate each \\(\\ell_i\\) right by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); denote the translated lines by \\(\\ell_i'\\).  \n> Because parallelism is preserved under translation, each \\(\\ell_i'\\) is still sunny.”\n\n*Correct.* Translation preserves slope, so sunny property is retained.\n\n*Step 3 – Distinctness*  \n\n> “The vertical lines are pairwise distinct.  \n> The sunny lines \\(\\ell_i'\\) are distinct because the original \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct and translation is a bijection on lines.  \n> No vertical line coincides with a sunny line, because a sunny line is never parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n> Hence the total number of distinct lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).”\n\n*Correct.* All lines are distinct; vertical lines cannot equal a non‑vertical line.\n\n*Step 4 – Coverage*  \n\n> “Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n> – If \\(a\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n> – If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\). Applying \\(\\tau\\) sends \\((a,b)\\) to \\((a-(n-k),b)\\in T_k\\), which is covered by some \\(\\ell_i\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding translated line \\(\\ell_i'\\).  \n> Thus every required point belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.”\n\n*Correct.* Directly uses Lemma 2 and the covering of \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Step 5 – Special case \\(k=0\\)*  \n\n> “When \\(k=0\\) the construction uses only the vertical lines (3) with \\(n-k=n\\); there are no sunny lines. Since every point of \\(T_n\\) has first coordinate \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).”\n\n*Correct.* Handles the edge case cleanly.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\n> “Any admissible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines is a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\). Therefore the only possible values are \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\). The construction above shows that each of these values is attainable; consequently the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).”\n\n*Correct.* The problem statement requires exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative. The previous construction proves attainability of every integer in that range.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Conclusion\n\n> “For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is \\(\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}\\).”\n\n*Correct.* This matches the result established by the construction and the impossibility argument.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\nThe solution correctly solves the problem. The only shortcomings are two minor **Justification Gaps** (the preservation of distinctness after translation in Lemma 1 and the explicit bijection in Lemma 2). These gaps are easily filled with routine arguments and do not undermine the correctness of the final answer. Consequently, the solution is deemed **correct**."
