[\n   (a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau(U_{n,k})=T_k .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines:* take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (V). They are distinct and each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.  \n\n   *Sunny lines:* by Lemma\u202f1, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\). Translating each \\(\\ell_i\\) right by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) gives sunny lines \\(\\ell_i'\\) that cover precisely the set \\(U_{n,k}\\).  \n\n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(\\ell_i'\\)) are sunny.  \n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (V).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\); applying the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) puts the point in \\(T_k\\), which is covered by some \\(\\ell_i\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding translated line \\(\\ell_i'\\).  \n\n   Thus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\). Consequently the only possible values are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\). The construction above shows that each of these values is attainable (the case \\(k=0\\) uses only the vertical lines (V), the case \\(k=n\\) uses only the sunny lines from Lemma\u202f1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad m\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(T_m\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(m,1),(1,m)\\); for \\(m=0\\) we have \\(T_0=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Sunny covering of a triangle, including the empty case)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) there exist exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n- **Base case \\(m=0\\).**  \\(T_0=\\varnothing\\); the empty family of lines (zero lines) trivially covers it, and \\(0\\) is the required number.\n\n- **Base case \\(m=1\\).**  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). Choose any line through \\((1,1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny and covers the single point.\n\n- **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the statement holds for some \\(m-1\\ge 1\\).  \n  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_m:\\; y=(m-1)x+1 . \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(m-1\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L_m\\) is sunny.  \n  Substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=m\\), so \\((1,m)\\in L_m\\).  \n\n  If \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lay on \\(L_m\\), then  \n\n  \\[\n  b=(m-1)a+1\\ge (m-1)\\cdot2+1=2m-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  But any point of \\(T_m\\) satisfies \\(b\\le m+1-a\\le m-1\\) when \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n  Hence no point of \\(T_m\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) belongs to \\(L_m\\); consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  T_m\\cap L_m=\\{(1,m)\\}. \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  Removing the first column of \\(T_m\\) and shifting the remaining points one unit left yields  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid a\\ge 2\\}\n   =\\{(a-1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{m-1}\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  By the induction hypothesis, \\(T_{m-1}\\) can be covered by \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines; translating those lines one unit to the right gives \\(m-1\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n\n  Adding the line \\(L_m\\) (which covers the remaining point \\((1,m)\\) by (2)) we obtain a family of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering every point of \\(T_m\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Reduction to a smaller triangle)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct and each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence not sunny.\n\n*Proof of coverage of a part of \\(T_n\\).*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from (3).  \n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by (3) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the translation \\(\\tau:(a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\).  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\) we have \\(1\\le a-(n-k)\\le k\\) and  \n\n\\[\n(a-(n-k))+b = a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\tau(U_{n,k})=T_k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3).  \n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  \n   Translate each \\(\\ell_i\\) right by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); denote the translated lines by \\(\\ell_i'\\).  \n   Because parallelism is preserved under translation, each \\(\\ell_i'\\) is still sunny.  \n\n3. **Verification that the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.**  \n   The vertical lines are pairwise distinct.  \n   The sunny lines \\(\\ell_i'\\) are distinct because the original \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct and translation is a bijection on lines.  \n   No vertical line coincides with a sunny line, because a sunny line is never parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n   Hence the total number of distinct lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).\n\n4. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (3).  \n\n   - If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\in U_{n,k}\\). Applying \\(\\tau\\) sends \\((a,b)\\) to \\((a-(n-k),b)\\in T_k\\), which is covered by some \\(\\ell_i\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding translated line \\(\\ell_i'\\).  \n\n   Thus every point required by the problem statement belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\n5. **Special case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   When \\(k=0\\) the construction uses only the vertical lines (3) with \\(n-k=n\\); there are no sunny lines.  \n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) has first coordinate \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Hence the case \\(k=0\\) is fully covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines is a non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\). Therefore the only possible values are  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nThe construction above shows that each of these values is attainable; consequently the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
